This assert checks that if the return value is false (which means the x is not in the list), then the last position of x in the new list should be -1.
--------------------Below is the output corresponding to the comments that start with "@@@" in the provided C# code. I've used the `Debug.Assert` method in the place of the comments for this purpose.

```cs
namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    using System.Diagnostics;
    using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;
    using System;
    using ArrayList;

    [TestFixture, PexClass]
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_ContainsContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            // Obtain the Old values before the operation
            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            // Perform the operation
            bool New_Ret = arrList.Contains(x);

            // Obtain the New values after the operation
            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            // Assert statements
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount);
            Debug.Assert(New_Ret == !Old_arrListContainsX);
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= 0 || Old_arrListLastIndexOfX < 0);
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListContainsX == (New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= 0));
        }
    }
}
```
Please note that the `Debug.Assert` statements are only valid if the program is run with the `DEFINE_ASSERTS` symbol defined. This is typically done in debug configurations. The conditions in the assertions are assumed based on the provided comments, and might not be 100% correct.
